WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.710
Hi friends!

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:02.064
Remember one hundred?

00:00:02.064 --> 00:00:05.677
Today we're meeting an even BIGGER number: one thousand!

00:00:05.677 --> 00:00:08.193
We write it with a one and three zeros.

00:00:08.193 --> 00:00:10.773
Let's discover what makes it so special!

00:00:11.473 --> 00:00:13.015
Let's count by hundreds!

00:00:13.015 --> 00:00:17.382
One hundred, two hundred, three hundred, four hundred, five hundred,

00:00:17.382 --> 00:00:21.877
six hundred, seven hundred, eight hundred, nine hundred, one thousand!

00:00:21.877 --> 00:00:23.868
Ten hundreds make one thousand.

00:00:24.468 --> 00:00:28.178
Build a cube with ten little blocks across, ten blocks

00:00:28.178 --> 00:00:29.964
back, and ten layers high.

00:00:29.964 --> 00:00:31.613
Ten times ten times ten.

00:00:31.613 --> 00:00:33.812
That's one thousand tiny blocks!

00:00:34.412 --> 00:00:37.477
Words that start with kilo mean one thousand.

00:00:37.477 --> 00:00:39.862
A kilometre is one thousand metres.

00:00:39.862 --> 00:00:41.156
That's a long walk!

00:00:41.156 --> 00:00:44.698
And a kilogram is one thousand grams, about as heavy

00:00:44.698 --> 00:00:46.401
as a big bottle of water.

00:00:47.001 --> 00:00:48.672
Milli goes the other way.

00:00:48.672 --> 00:00:52.215
Chop a metre into one thousand teeny pieces, and each

00:00:52.215 --> 00:00:53.552
one is a millimetre.

00:00:53.552 --> 00:00:56.493
And one second is one thousand milliseconds.

00:00:56.493 --> 00:00:56.894
Blink!

00:00:56.894 --> 00:00:59.033
That took a few hundred of them.

00:00:59.633 --> 00:01:02.819
A thousand years has a special name: a millennium!

00:01:02.819 --> 00:01:06.068
From the year one thousand to the year two thousand

00:01:06.068 --> 00:01:07.661
was one whole millennium.

00:01:07.661 --> 00:01:08.999
That's ten centuries!

00:01:09.599 --> 00:01:11.698
Millipede means thousand feet.

00:01:11.698 --> 00:01:15.615
But most millipedes have way fewer than a thousand legs!

00:01:15.615 --> 00:01:20.583
Scientists did find one tiny millipede with over thirteen hundred legs.

00:01:20.583 --> 00:01:20.863
Wow!

00:01:21.463 --> 00:01:24.866
If you counted to one thousand, saying one number every

00:01:24.866 --> 00:01:27.713
second, it would take about seventeen minutes!

00:01:27.713 --> 00:01:30.807
And one thousand days is almost three whole years.

00:01:31.407 --> 00:01:34.739
Long ago, the Romans wrote numbers with letters.

00:01:34.739 --> 00:01:37.447
To them, one thousand was the letter M.

00:01:37.447 --> 00:01:39.044
So M is for a thousand!

00:01:39.644 --> 00:01:43.066
So remember: ten hundreds, a ten by ten by ten

00:01:43.066 --> 00:01:47.679
cube, kilometres, millimetres, a millennium, and the letter M.

00:01:47.679 --> 00:01:49.465
One thousand is amazing.

00:01:49.465 --> 00:01:51.548
Great job, number explorers!
